You’re right to push back—the earlier statement was too broad. Java is always **pass-by-value**, but when the value being copied is an object reference, both references can point to the same mutable object.

There are two different things we might mean by “swap”:

1. **Swap the caller’s references**  
   Make `x` point to the original `y` object and `y` point to the original `x` object.
2. **Swap the contents of the objects**  
   Keep `x` and `y` pointing to the same objects, but exchange the objects’ internal values.

The first is impossible with only the two parameter variables. The second is possible when the objects are mutable.

## Example 1: Mutable objects

```java
public final class Box<T> {
    private T value;

    public Box(T value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    public T get() {
        return value;
    }

    public void set(T value) {
        this.value = value;
    }
}
```

Now this method works:

```java
public void swap(Box<String> a, Box<String> b) {
    String temp = a.get();
    a.set(b.get());
    b.set(temp);
}
```

Example:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    Box<String> x = new Box<>("X");
    Box<String> y = new Box<>("Y");

    new SwapDemo().swap(x, y);

    System.out.println(x.get()); // Y
    System.out.println(y.get()); // X
}
```

Before the call:

```text
x ──────> Box("X")
y ──────> Box("Y")

a ──────> Box("X")
b ──────> Box("Y")
```

The `a` and `b` parameters contain copies of the references, but those references still point to the same boxes. Calling `a.set(...)` changes the actual box, so the caller sees the change.

Afterward:

```text
x and a ──> Box("Y")
y and b ──> Box("X")
```

The important distinction is:

- `a.set(...)` changes an object.
- `a = b` only changes the local parameter variable.

## Why simply swapping object references still fails

This method would not work:

```java
public void swapReferences(Box<String> a, Box<String> b) {
    Box<String> temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp;
}
```

The caller would still see:

```text
x ──> original X box
y ──> original Y box
```

The method swapped only its own local references `a` and `b`. When the method returns, those local references disappear.

Likewise, this would not work:

```java
public void swap(String a, String b) {
    String temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp;
}
```

`String` is immutable, and this code only reassigns the local references anyway.

## Example 2: Mutable reference containers

Java’s `AtomicReference` is a built-in mutable container:

```java
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicReference;

public void swap(AtomicReference<String> a,
                 AtomicReference<String> b) {
    String temp = a.get();
    a.set(b.get());
    b.set(temp);
}
```

This works because `a.set(...)` and `b.set(...)` change the contents of objects that the caller also has references to.

## The precise rule

| Operation | Does the caller see a change? |
|---|---:|
| Reassigning an `int` parameter | No |
| Reassigning an object parameter | No |
| Changing a mutable object’s field through a parameter | Yes |
| Changing an array element through a parameter | Yes |

Therefore, with the original signature:

```java
public void swap(int a, int b)
```

a `void` method cannot make the caller’s two primitive variables exchange values. But with mutable object parameters, a method can exchange the objects’ internal values while keeping the original object identities unchanged.

If the goal is truly to change the caller’s references from `x → A, y → B` into `x → B, y → A`, then either:

- the caller must perform the assignments,
- the method must return the values, or
- the references must be stored inside mutable containers such as `Box<T>` or `AtomicReference<T>`.

