At a dead end, the previous cell is still `1`, so the method repeatedly walks back and forth instead of returning `false`. If the dead end happens to lie on the last row or column, the bad loop condition may instead cause it to return `true`.

### One correct iterative approach

A queue-based search is valid and naturally ignores disconnected trails:

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public boolean isTraversable(int[][] mountain) {
    if (mountain == null ||
        mountain.length == 0 ||
        mountain[0].length == 0) {
        return false;
    }

    int rows = mountain.length;
    int cols = mountain[0].length;

    if (mountain[0][0] != 1 ||
        mountain[rows - 1][cols - 1] != 1) {
        return false;
    }

    Deque<int[]> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
    boolean[][] visited = new boolean[rows][cols];

    queue.add(new int[]{0, 0});
    visited[0][0] = true;

    int[][] directions = {
        {1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}
    };

    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
        int[] position = queue.poll();
        int row = position[0];
        int col = position[1];

        if (row == rows - 1 && col == cols - 1) {
            return true;
        }

        for (int[] direction : directions) {
            int nextRow = row + direction[0];
            int nextCol = col + direction[1];

            if (nextRow >= 0 && nextRow < rows &&
                nextCol >= 0 && nextCol < cols &&
                !visited[nextRow][nextCol] &&
                mountain[nextRow][nextCol] == 1) {
                visited[nextRow][nextCol] = true;
                queue.add(new int[]{nextRow, nextCol});
            }
        }
    }

    return false;
