### A correct implementation

Under the usual left-to-right, non-overlapping interpretation of occurrences, the layers can be processed as follows:

```java
public int numNestedOccurrences(String string, String word) {
    if (word == null || word.isEmpty()) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("word must not be empty");
    }

    int total = 0;
    String current = string;

    while (true) {
        StringBuilder next = new StringBuilder();
        int foundThisLayer = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < current.length(); ) {
            if (current.startsWith(word, i)) {
                foundThisLayer++;
                i += word.length();
            } else {
                next.append(current.charAt(i));
                i++;
            }
        }

        if (foundThisLayer == 0) {
            return total;
        }

        total += foundThisLayer;
        current = next.toString();
    }
}
```

This version:

- adds one per full occurrence;
- preserves every unmatched character;
- tests every possible starting position;
- never repeats the same index without making progress;
- performs one complete layer before looking for newly exposed nested occurrences;
- avoids deep recursion.

The student's `if (count == 0) return count;` stopping condition is conceptually reasonable for a correctly scanned layer, but it cannot compensate for the scanning and counting errors above.

