## Correct implementation

```java
public static Node removeKthFromEnd(Node head, int k) {
    if (head == null || k <= 0) {
        return head;
    }

    Node fast = head;
    Node slow = head;

    // Move fast k - 1 steps ahead.
    for (int i = 1; i < k && fast != null; i++) {
        fast = fast.next;
    }

    // k is greater than the number of nodes.
    if (fast == null) {
        return head;
    }

    // Position slow immediately before the node to remove.
    while (fast.next != null) {
        fast = fast.next;
        slow = slow.next;
    }

    // The target is the head, including a one-node list.
    if (slow == head) {
        return head.next;
    }

    slow.next = slow.next.next;
    return head;
}
```

This correctly handles:

- Empty list
- One-node list
- `k = 1`, removing the tail
- `k == list length`, removing the head
- `k > list length`, leaving the list unchanged
- Invalid nonpositive `k` under the chosen no-op policy

It also uses one forward two-pointer pass with \(O(n)\) time and \(O(1)\) auxiliary space.

